        }}
        .findings-list li {{
            margin-bottom: 15px;
        }}
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>UK Retail Fuel Price Trend Analysis — February 2026</h1>

    <div class="summary-box">
        <p><strong>One-Liner:</strong> A comprehensive analysis of retail fuel pricing patterns across UK forecourts, examining price distributions by fuel type and temporal update behaviour based on government-mandated open data.</p>
        <p><strong>Goal:</strong> Provide policy analysts and energy researchers with a clear understanding of current UK fuel price levels by fuel type, the distribution and spread of prices across stations, and how frequently retailers update their reported prices — enabling evidence-based assessment of market competitiveness and pricing transparency.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="kpi-container">
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-label">Unique Station Count</div>
            <div class="kpi-value">{unique_stations:,}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-label">Total Records</div>
            <div class="kpi-value">{total_records:,}</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-label">Median Update Lag</div>
            <div class="kpi-value">{overall_median_lag:.1f} hrs</div>
        </div>
        <div class="kpi-card">
            <div class="kpi-label">Date Range</div>
            <div class="kpi-value" style="font-size: 16px;">{min_date} to {max_date}</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="overview">Dataset Overview & Coverage Summary</h2>
    <p>This report analyses fuel price records sourced from the UK Government's official Fuel Finder API, aggregated by fuelcosts.co.uk. The dataset encompasses <strong>{total_records:,}</strong> individual price observations across <strong>{unique_stations:,}</strong> distinct forecourts. The reporting period spans from <strong>{min_date}</strong> to <strong>{max_date}</strong>. The dataset covers {len(fuel_types)} distinct fuel types: {', '.join(fuel_types)}.</p>

    <h2 id="price-by-fuel-type">Price Distribution by Fuel Type</h2>
    <p>The table below presents the core statistical distribution of prices across all reported fuel types. The data demonstrates significant variance in average prices depending on the fuel grade.</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Fuel Type</th>
                <th>Count</th>
                <th>Mean (p)</th>
                <th>Median (p)</th>
                <th>Min (p)</th>
                <th>Max (p)</th>
                <th>Std Dev (p)</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {''.join([f"<tr><td>{row['fuel_type']}</td><td>{int(row['count']):,}</td><td>{row['mean']:.2f}</td><td>{row['median']:.2f}</td><td>{row['min']:.2f}</td><td>{row['max']:.2f}</td><td>{row['std']:.2f}</td></tr>" for _, row in price_stats.iterrows()])}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <img src="data:image/png;base64,{img_price_dist}" alt="Price Distribution Boxplot">
        <p><em>Figure 1: Box plot showing the distribution, median, and outliers for each fuel type.</em></p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="price-spread-analysis">Price Spread & Market Competitiveness</h2>
    <p>To assess market competitiveness, we examine the price spread (Max minus Min) and the Interquartile Range (IQR) for each fuel type. A narrow IQR suggests high competitiveness and transparent pricing for the majority of consumers, while a wide absolute spread indicates isolated forecourts applying significant premiums (often motorway stations or remote locations).</p>

    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Fuel Type</th>
                <th>IQR (p)</th>
                <th>Price Spread (Max - Min) (p)</th>
                <th>Q1 (25th Percentile) (p)</th>
                <th>Q3 (75th Percentile) (p)</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {''.join([f"<tr><td>{row['fuel_type']}</td><td>{row['IQR']:.2f}</td><td>{row['spread']:.2f}</td><td>{row['Q1']:.2f}</td><td>{row['Q3']:.2f}</td></tr>" for _, row in price_stats.iterrows()])}
        </tbody>
    </table>

